      "value": "@{5f2e2ab863aa6f7311d53d03|full_name}: I have figured out why VR chat crashes so much it’s because it’s using way too much memory on the quest and if you’re in a lobby by yourself you have about 700 MB free of memory, but is soon as you go into a lobby with anyone else it drops to about 200 to 300 MB and if it goes below around 50 to 80 MB the game will crash. Sometimes the quest can recover but not always.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-09-22T19:21:02.397Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I'll describe the specific sequence that happens with this crash and the workaround we're using.\n1. Without warning, in any world, instance, regardless of the load on the system. The game will suddenly freeze. This sometimes leads into the game resuming, but often leads into the HMD freezing up.\n2. The HMD will show the last two frames repeatedly in a quick flicker. The user is still able to talk and listen to users around them indefinitely, the client never crashes.\n3. The only thing the user can do is restart VRChat to regain visuals on their HMD.\nWe currently cannot identify this by hardware, in our pool of people we've had various combinations of AMD/Intel CPUs and AMD/Nvdia GPUs.\nBy increasing the TdrDelay and TdrDdiDelay registry values to a higher number (we picked 15), the game resumes instead of the HMD flickering the two frames up after the initial freeze.\nTo put it simple, TdrDelay is the amount of time that windows waits before restarting an unresponsive GPU. \nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/display/tdr-registry-keys\nThis issue is as common as over 80% of oculus users within my circle. All these users reported zero new HMD freezes post the initial freeze after adjusting Tdr values.",
